      "id": "hook",
      "title": "hook: salt and rust",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Last time, sodium lost an electron to become a plus one ion, and chlorine gained one to become minus one. Put them together and you get salt, sodium chloride, with one sodium ion for every chloride ion. Rust is different. It contains an iron oxide with two irons for every three oxygens. Why those numbers? The answer is in the charges on the ions."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "why-ions",
      "title": "why atoms form the ions they do",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "First, why does each atom form the ion it does? Atoms of the main groups tend to lose or gain electrons until they have the same arrangement as the nearest noble gas, with a full outer shell. Sodium is two, eight, one. Losing that one outer electron leaves two, eight, like neon, so sodium forms a plus one ion. Oxygen is two, six. Gaining two electrons makes two, eight, so oxygen forms an oxide ion, with a charge of minus two. The charge is just protons minus electrons."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "group-charges",
      "title": "the group decides the charge",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Because atoms in a group have the same number of outer electrons, they form ions with the same charge. Groups one, two and thirteen lose one, two or three electrons, so their ions are plus one, plus two and plus three. Groups fifteen, sixteen and seventeen gain three, two or one, so they form minus three, minus two and minus one. Metals form positive ions, called cations. Non-metals form negative ions, called anions. Group fourteen usually shares electrons instead, and noble gases usually stay neutral."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "neutrality",
      "title": "the rule: no overall charge",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Now the key rule. An ionic compound is made of positive and negative ions, but as a whole, it has no overall charge. A grain of salt isn't charged. So the ions must combine in a ratio where the positive charges exactly cancel the negative ones. Sodium is plus one, chloride is minus one, so one of each. Magnesium is plus two, so it needs two chloride ions to cancel it. That's M g C l two. The small number written after a symbol, called a subscript, tells you how many of that ion are in the formula."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "aluminium-oxide",
      "title": "aluminium oxide",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Let's try a harder pair, aluminium and oxygen. Aluminium is in group thirteen, so it forms plus three ions. Oxide is minus two. We need the total positive charge to equal the total negative charge. The smallest number that both three and two go into is six. So two aluminium ions give plus six, and three oxide ions give minus six. The formula is A l two O three. Check: plus six and minus six make zero."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "shortcut",
      "title": "a shortcut, and simplest ratio",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "There's a quicker way to the same answer. Write the two charges, then cross them over, so each ion's charge number becomes the other ion's subscript. Aluminium three and oxygen two give A l two O three. But always simplify. Magnesium plus two and oxide minus two cross over to M g two O two, which cancels down to M g O. A formula shows the simplest ratio of ions. Then check that the charges add to zero. Cross over, cancel down, check for zero."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
